From aaeb5d84e606ac70795b8a0599b85d63023a849c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 16:55:32 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] parse: an associated item constraint's bounds take lifetimes

rustc 1.90 tests/ui (parse-only run, conformance/results/parse-20261118)
failed on a lifetime in the bound list of `Assoc: Bounds` inside
generic arguments:

    associated-type-bounds/bounds-on-assoc-in-trait.rs
        type B: Iterator<Item: 'static>;
    associated-type-bounds/issue-70292.rs   F: for<'a> Trait<Output: 'a>,
    associated-type-bounds/issue-71443-2.rs for<'a> F: Iterator<Item: 'a> + 'b,
    impl-trait/in-trait/lifetime-in-associated-trait-bound.rs
        fn method(&self) -> impl Trait<Type: '_>;
    error: Unexpected token TOK_LIFETIME, expected TOK_IDENT

Reference, Paths > GenericArgsBounds: `IDENTIFIER GenericArgs? :
TypeParamBounds`, and TypeParamBound is `Lifetime | TraitBound |
UseBound`. Parse_Path_GenericList read every bound as a trait path.

A lifetime in that list is now consumed and not kept: the AST entry
holds trait paths only, and lifetimes are not checked. A list of only
lifetimes adds no entry (`I: Iterator<Item: 'static>` is `I: Iterator`
from then on); a trait next to a lifetime is kept and still applies.

Grammar copies: the parser is the only one that splits the list. The
macro_rules skimmer reads generic arguments as angle-bracketed token
trees (checked with `$t:ty` and a `tt` repetition); the proc-macro
visitor and ast/dump print the entry they get.

Twelve more associated-type-bounds tests now stop at a higher-ranked
trait bound in the same list (`Item: for<'a> Tr<'a>`), which the AST
entry cannot hold yet.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5
